      "text": " The null hypothesis states that the observed percentages of obese individuals in each age class are equal to the expected percentages from the national average. The alternative hypothesis suggests that there is a difference between the observed and expected percentages. The chi-square (χ2) test statistic is calculated by comparing the observed and expected frequencies in each cell of the contingency table. The degrees of freedom are calculated as (k - 1)(k - 2) / 2, where k is the number of categories. The critical value of χ2 is determined from the chi-square distribution table for the desired significance level (α = 0.05). The decision to reject or fail to reject the null hypothesis is based on whether the calculated test statistic is greater than the critical value. The conclusion is that the observed data significantly differ from the expected data, indicating that the survey participants are not a representative sample of the USA obese population."
